<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Belgrade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1739</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-russian-turkish-war-1735-1739">Austro-Russian–Turkish War (1735–1739)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, signed in September 1739, ended the war of 1735–1739 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. After a badly managed campaign, Austria returned Belgrade, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>, which it had gained at Passarowitz in 1718. In a parallel settlement Russia kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> but had to demolish its fortifications and keep no fleet on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and Kabarda was declared a neutral barrier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-belgrade.jpg" alt="Treaty of Belgrade" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belgrade_Treaty_1739.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
